snippet.textDisplay:
@djrussell1989 All good points.  I fear that the numbers do not favor even the possibility of a civilized, fair society, though.  Power is attained by many means, but Evil gets ahead by convincing enough people that it is just the opposite.  However, we are entering an age, for the first time in known history, where the most powerful will not even need any support from the public to implement their agenda. At the same time, who needs an army when you've convinced enough of the public that the REAL enemy is the guy next door, down the street, in a different city, or from a different country?  Who needs a judicial warrant to bug a place when you can access information from the person's phone, computer, tv and in some cases, even toilets?  Who needs a trial when a person's own car can be made to drive him off a cliff?  Who needs a job when AI has replaced it?  Who needs paper money when it all goes digital and bank accounts can be wiped out, or purchases denied, automatically via decisions made by AI?

Even if we could agree on the problem, causes, and what SHOULD be done -- and even if we could find good, intelligent, wealthy people to rally arouind -- we would be basically reduced to throwing little rocks and snowballs at a row of unmanned, automated tanks that extends as far as the eye can see. 

Guess we'll see if I'm right. Inaction is not an option, but to me it seems that we are half a century late in waking up.

