snippet.textDisplay:
@calculatedrisk1  Yep, horrible advice. He also uses the line “drink more and make bad decisions, they might pay off”. That advice feels outdated. Maybe that sounded edgy before smartphones, but now everyone has a camera, everything can be clipped, uploaded, shared, searched and taken out of context.

A drunken mistake is not always just a funny story anymore. It can become part of your digital footprint. Employers already use tools like Fama and Ferretly for social media and online screening, and other hiring tools like HireVue have used AI in candidate assessment. Fama describes itself as AI-powered social media screening for employment, and Ferretly markets itself as AI-powered digital behaviour screening. These tools can scan public online content for risk signals, misconduct, reputation issues, hate speech, threats, or other red flags.

So telling young people to socialize more and take reasonable interpersonal risks is fine. Telling them to drink more and make bad decisions is reckless advice in a world where one stupid moment can follow you into hiring, dating, reputation and professional life.

